ÑThe Incarnation of God reaches its very peak of self-humiliation and self-abnegation in the mystery of the Eucharist. In this sacrament the mystery of the One Who is "God with us" reveals itself in an insuperable manner, with all the consequences of the incomparable love of God.
Christ is not only "God with us". He is God, Who in the little sacred host has delivered Himself into the hands of men, completely renouncing His own defense. The Eucharistic Jesus in the sacred host is truly the One Who is the most poor and the most defenceless in the Church, and this is what He becomes firstly during the distribution of Communion.
During the distribution of Communion one notices in our days the phenomenon of a surprising lack of sensibility and care regarding all the concrete requirements that should accompany the true and substantial presence of God Incarnated in the little sacred host. The necessary exterior acts of adoration, of sacred gestures and of caring in treating the sacred host are often reduced to a minimum. The exterior manner of treating the sacred host with minimalistic gestures of adoration gradually diminishes faith in the Incarnation and in the Eucharistic Transubstantiation, which becomes limited to a theoretical area.
The Eucharist is the very heart of the Church's life. When, therefore, the concrete worship of the Eucharistic God is objectively reduced, the heart of the Church's life will be wounded. The heart of the Church's life in our days needs to be healed of the manner in which the Eucharistic Jesus in the sacred host is treated. For the sacred host - even in its minutest fragment - is nothing less than the Lord Himself."é
ãThe Russo-Turkish war is at a critical juncture, and Erast Fandorin, broken-hearted and disillusioned, has gone to the front in an attempt to forget his sorrows. But Fandorin's efforts to steer clear of trouble are thwarted when he comes to the aid of Varvara Suvorova - a 'progressive' Russian woman trying to make her way to the Russian headquarters to join her fiance. looms on the horizon, and there are rumours of a Turkish spy hiding within their own camp. Our reluctant gentleman sleuth will need to resurrect all of his dormant powers of detection if he is to unmask the traitor, help the Russians to victory and smooth the path of young love."ê	
ç	L'Historia Regum Britanniae narra in undici libri le gesta dei re dei Britanni, discendenti da Bruto, pronipote di Enea, che si insedia nell'isola dopo averla conquistata. Tra loro, la favolosa figura di Artu che con i suoi cavalieri crea un regno di uno splendore ineguagliabile. Dopo un periodo di inarrestabile declino, in seguito alle invasioni di Sassoni, Angli, Danesi e Norvegesi, nell'Armorica, cio in Bretagna, un gruppo di scampati trova rifugio: da qui partira la riscossa e la riconquista dell'isola.
L'Historia Regum Britanniae, che sappiamo essere gia pubblicata nel 1136, ebbe un grande successo; copiata in numerosi manoscritti e tradotta da Robert Wace nel 1155 in versi francesi, costituisce una delle fonti del romanzo bretone.
Il testo contiene anche i Detti di Merlino (l'attribuzione a Goffredo di Monmouth e controversa).
I Detti di Merlino furono composti tra il 1150 e il 1151; in essi si narra di un giovane principe che, sconvolto dagli orrori di una battaglia, visse a lungo nella foresta, tra fiere e animali, e di come conquisto e poi perse il dono della profezia. E il testo piu antico in cui compare il magico personaggio di Merlino."†
ùIn The Hotel Majestic, Maigret investigates the murder of Mrs. Clark, the wife of a wealthy American industrialist, whose strangled body is found in the basement of an upscale hotel near the Champs-Elysees. Maigret's inquiries take him from the endless corridors of the Hotel Majestic to the countryside of the Bois de Boulogne and sun-drenched Cannes, into a world of prostitution, drug addiction, and blackmail."•
